.TH FREXP 3
.SH NAME
frexp, ldexp, modf \- split into mantissa and exponent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.B double frexp(double value, int *eptr);
.PP
.B double ldexp(double value, int exp);
.PP
.B double modf(double value, double *iptr);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Frexp
returns the mantissa of a double
.I value
as a double quantity,
.I x,
of magnitude less than 1
and stores an integer
.I n
such that
.I value
=
.IR x *2** n
indirectly through
.I eptr.
.PP
.I Ldexp
returns the quantity
.IR value *2** exp.
.PP
.I Modf
returns the fractional part of
.I value
and stores the integer part indirectly
through
.I iptr.
.B Note:
v7's page said "positive fractional part".  Both parts carry the
.I sign of value,
which is the split C11 sec 7.12.6.12 requires and the one
.IR trunc (3)
and
.IR round (3)
are built on.
.SH SEE ALSO
exp(3), floor(3), ecvt(3), atof(3)
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS These three are in libc, not libm
They are the exponent surgery the
.I conversions
need \-
.IR atof (3),
.IR ecvt (3)
and the
.IR printf (3)
engine \- so a program gets them
.B without \-lm,
which is also where v7 keeps them.  <math.h> declares them under a banner saying
so.
.SS Both are exact, and libm leans on that
.I Frexp
is twelve instructions and
.I ldexp
six, ports of the external c-compiler's Madlen originals.  There is no
approximation in either: the exponent field is bits 48..42 of the word, biased by
64, so
.I frexp
shifts it down and masks and
.I ldexp
ORs a new one in and multiplies.
.PP
.B Ldexp does no range check whatever.
It keeps only the low seven bits of its argument, so an out-of-range
.I exp
aliases modulo 128 and the result is nonsense rather than an error.  That is what
.IR exp (3)'s
lower range gate stands between a caller and, and it is the reason that gate is
not cosmetic.
.SS modf knows where the fractional bits run out
Any magnitude at or above
.B 2**40
is already integral in a 40-bit mantissa, so
.I modf
returns a zero fraction there and the integer part unchanged.  Below that the
integer part fits in a 41-bit
.BR int ,
so a truncating round trip through
.B int
recovers it exactly.
.PP
That 2**40 is the constant
.IR sin (3)
and
.IR pow (3)
in libm key on by name, and it is defined here.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/frexp.s, ldexp.s, modf.c
